From: Greg Hudson <ghudson@mit.edu>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 21:44:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 64/90] Prevent null dereference in PKINIT CMS processing

In cms_signeddata_verify(), where we use CMS_get0_content(), check
that the dereferenced result is non-null as well as the result itself.
(The result itself is a pointer to the eContent slot, whether or not
the decoded ASN.1 value contained an eContent field, and will only be
null if the CMS_ContentInfo object has an unexpected type.)

CVE-2026-71200:

If a KDC is configured to allow PKINIT authentication, an
unauthenticated attacker can trigger a null pointer dereference,
causing the process to terminate.  Discovered by Claude and Ada
Logics, and independently by Erin Liman using DeepSeek.

ticket: 9227 (new)
tags: pullup
target_version: 1.22-next

(cherry picked from commit 30544af7da0a3469c3e58c2a1a5bdf8a97284717)
---
 src/plugins/preauth/pkinit/pkinit_crypto_openssl.c | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/plugins/preauth/pkinit/pkinit_crypto_openssl.c b/src/plugins/preauth/pkinit/pkinit_crypto_openssl.c
index ead6bca44f..b85d31b5e0 100644
--- a/src/plugins/preauth/pkinit/pkinit_crypto_openssl.c
+++ b/src/plugins/preauth/pkinit/pkinit_crypto_openssl.c
@@ -1988,7 +1988,8 @@ cms_signeddata_verify(krb5_context context,
         unsigned char *d;
         *is_signed = 0;
         octets = CMS_get0_content(cms);
-        if (!octets || ((*octets)->type != V_ASN1_OCTET_STRING)) {
+        if (octets == NULL || *octets == NULL ||
+            ((*octets)->type != V_ASN1_OCTET_STRING)) {
             retval = KRB5KDC_ERR_PREAUTH_FAILED;
             krb5_set_error_message(context, retval,
                                    _("Invalid pkinit packet: octet string "
@@ -2043,7 +2044,7 @@ cms_signeddata_verify(krb5_context context,
         *is_signed = 0;
         /* We cannot use CMS_dataInit because there may be no digest */
         octets = CMS_get0_content(cms);
-        if (octets)
+        if (octets != NULL && *octets != NULL)
             out = BIO_new_mem_buf((*octets)->data, (*octets)->length);
         if (out == NULL)
             goto cleanup;
